13. Comparing with the Do-Nothing Baseline

I learned why a machine-learning model should be compared with a simple baseline.

The exercise used a baseline where the model predicts that nobody churns.

The baseline accuracy was calculated using:

baseline_accuracy = 1 - y_test.mean()

This demonstrated that accuracy can sometimes appear high simply because the majority class is larger.

Therefore, a high accuracy value alone does not prove that a churn model is useful.

14. Understanding Decision Thresholds

I learned that a classification model produces probabilities of churn, which are then converted into class predictions using a decision threshold.

For example:

Probability of churn = 0.72

At a threshold of 0.5, this subscriber would be classified as likely to churn.

However, changing the threshold changes how many subscribers are flagged.

The exercise tested thresholds from 0.10 to 0.60 in increments of 0.05.

15. Understanding Precision and Recall Trade-off

I learned that changing the decision threshold affects precision and recall.

A lower threshold generally flags more subscribers as potential churners.

This can increase recall because more actual churners may be identified, but it can also increase false positives.

A higher threshold generally flags fewer subscribers.

This can reduce false positives but may cause more actual churners to be missed.

Therefore, the threshold should be selected according to the actual objective of the business.

16. Selecting a Threshold Using Expected Cost

The exercise used the business costs of false negatives and false positives to select an operating point.

The expected cost was calculated using:

Expected Cost =
(False Negatives × ₹5,880)
+
(False Positives × ₹300)

The expected cost was calculated for each threshold in the sweep.

The threshold with the lowest expected cost under the stated assumptions can then be considered for the retention strategy.

I learned that the most suitable threshold for a business problem is not necessarily the default 0.5.

